nk:href=\"#E5-MJMAIN-32\"></use>\n</g>\n</g>\n</g>\n</g>\n</g>\n</g>\n<g transform=\"translate(2435,0)\">\n<g fill=\"blue\" stroke=\"blue\">\n<g fill=\"blue\" stroke=\"blue\">\n<g fill=\"blue\" stroke=\"blue\">\n<g fill=\"blue\" stroke=\"blue\">\n<g fill=\"blue\" stroke=\"blue\">\n <use xlink:href=\"#E5-MJMATHI-61\"></use>\n</g>\n</g>\n</g>\n</g>\n</g>\n</g>\n <use xlink:href=\"#E5-MJMAIN-2B\" x=\"3186\" y=\"0\"></use>\n <use xlink:href=\"#E5-MJMAIN-31\" x=\"4187\" y=\"0\"></use>\n</g>\n</svg></span>\nwhere <span class=\"mjpage\"><svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"1.23ex\" height=\"1.676ex\" style=\"vertical-align: -0.338ex;\" viewBox=\"0 -576.1 529.5 721.6\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" aria-labelledby=\"MathJax-SVG-6-Title\">\n<title id=\"MathJax-SVG-6-Title\">\\color{blue}a</title>\n<defs aria-hidden=\"true\">\n<path stroke-width=\"1\" id=\"E6-MJMATHI-61\" d=\"M33 157Q33 258 109 349T280 441Q331 441 370 392Q386 422 416 422Q429 422 439 414T449 394Q449 381 412 234T374 68Q374 43 381 35T402 26Q411 27 422 35Q443 55 463 131Q469 151 473 152Q475 153 483 153H487Q506 153 506 144Q506 138 501 117T481 63T449 13Q436 0 417 -8Q409 -10 393 -10Q359 -10 336 5T306 36L300 51Q299 52 296 50Q294 48 292 46Q233 -10 172 -10Q117 -10 75 30T33 157ZM351 328Q351 334 346 350T323 385T277 405Q242 405 210 374T160 293Q131 214 119 129Q119 126 119 118T118 106Q118 61 136 44T179 26Q217 26 254 59T298 110Q300 114 325 217T351 328Z\"></path>\n</defs>\n<g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n<g fill=\"blue\" stroke=\"blue\">\n<g fill=\"blue\" stroke=\"blue\">\n<g fill=\"blue\" stroke=\"blue\">\n <use xlink:href=\"#E6-MJMATHI-61\"></use>\n</g>\n</g>\n</g>\n</g>\n</svg></span> is an integer.</p>\n\n        </div>",
        "errors": [],
        "assets": [],
        "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14108275/observations/lesson-step-154001-06849586bb8e77d29e18.html",
        "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14108275/observations/lesson-step-154001-06849586bb8e77d29e18.png",
        "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/topics/3411",
        "captured_at": 1791694627.7228782,
        "math_academy_id": 8324,
        "title": "Representing Odd Integers",
        "content": "Recall that an integer is *odd* if it is not even. The list below gives the first few odd integers:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1, \\quad \\pm 3, \\quad \\pm 5, \\quad \\pm 7, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nNotice that every odd integer can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}\\times {\\color{blue}{\\textrm{some other integer}}} + 1.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =2\\times 0+1 \\\\ 3 & =2\\times 1+1 \\\\ 5 & =2\\times 2+1 \\\\ 19 & =2\\times 9+1 \\\\ -17 & =2\\times (-9)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, an integer $n$ is odd if it can be written as \n\n$$\nn={\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}+1\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}a$ is an integer."
      },
      {
        "dom_id": "step-154002",
        "name": "Showing a Statement Is Odd",
        "problem": "Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P:\\: \\forall n\\in\\mathbb Z,\\, 4n+7$ *is odd.*\n\nTo prove this statement, we need to show that it can be written as $2{\\color{blue}{a}}+1,$ where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer. \n\nThe first step is to rewrite the expression by isolating $+1.$ We can do this by decomposing $7$ into $6+1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4n+7 = 4n+6+1 = (4n+6)+1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out $2$ from the expression in parentheses:\n\n$$\n(4n+6)+1 = 2(2n+3) + 1\n$$\n\nThen, we introduce a new variable ${\\color{blue}{a}},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{a}} = 2n+3.\n$$\n\nNotice that since $n\\in\\mathbb Z,$ we have that $2n+3\\in\\mathbb Z.$ In other words, ${\\color{blue}{a}}\\in\\mathbb Z.$ \n\nThis means our original expression can be written as\n\n$$\n2(2n+3) + 1= 2{\\color{blue}{a}} + 1.\n$$\n\nSo, since $4n+7$ can be written in the form $2{\\color{blue}{a}}+1$ where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer, we conclude that $4n+7$ is odd *for all* $n\\in\\mathbb Z.$",
        "worked_solution": "",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
        "fields": [],
